---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-smoking`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Smoking',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Smoking', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Smoking: Why Smokers Have Lower 25(OH)D and Worse Bone Density"
  description="How smoking lowers 25(OH)D, what happens after quitting, and why smokers have accelerated bone loss and higher fracture risk on top of their vitamin D deficit."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-smoking", label: "Vitamin D and Smoking" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Smoking</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Smokers have consistently lower serum 25(OH)D than never-smokers — roughly
      3–5 ng/mL less on average, independent of sun exposure, BMI, and diet. This
      compounds their well-known bone-density and fracture risk. Quitting
      partially reverses the vitamin D difference; correcting deficiency is
      standard care for smokers.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Observational data</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      NHANES and European cohorts consistently show smokers have lower 25(OH)D.
      The effect is dose-dependent — heavy smokers (&gt; 20 cigarettes/day) show
      larger reductions than light smokers. Ex-smokers have intermediate levels;
      levels rise partially over the years after quitting.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Possible mechanisms</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Increased 25(OH)D catabolism from CYP450 induction by tobacco combustion products.</li>
      <li>Reduced hepatic 25-hydroxylation.</li>
      <li>Reduced physical activity and outdoor time in some smoking populations.</li>
      <li>Lower dietary calcium and vitamin D intake in smokers on average.</li>
      <li>Increased oxidative stress accelerating vitamin D breakdown.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bone consequences compound</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Smoking is an independent risk factor for osteoporosis via multiple mechanisms
      — direct effects on osteoblast function, altered estrogen metabolism (earlier
      menopause in women smokers), reduced calcium absorption, and cortisol effects.
      Combined with vitamin D deficiency, smokers have hip fracture risk ~40%
      higher than never-smokers. Correcting vitamin D deficiency is one modifiable
      component in this risk stack.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Passive smoking</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Some studies find lower 25(OH)D in non-smokers with significant passive
      smoke exposure, particularly children. The effect is smaller than active
      smoking. Reducing household smoking exposure benefits kids on many
      dimensions including bone health.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Vaping</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Data on vaping and vitamin D is limited. Nicotine effects on hepatic
      metabolism may be similar to smoking; combustion products are absent.
      Studies to date are inconclusive; the general recommendation is not to
      vape as a "safer alternative" for vitamin D reasons.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical guide</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Smokers: test 25(OH)D and correct any deficiency with standard doses.</li>
      <li>Ensure adequate calcium (1,000–1,200 mg/day) and consider DEXA screening earlier than non-smokers.</li>
      <li>Support smoking cessation — the vitamin D benefit is a small bonus to the many other reasons.</li>
      <li>Quitting resources: nicotine replacement, varenicline, bupropion, counselling, quitlines (US 1-800-QUIT-NOW).</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Smoking cessation is the single most
      valuable modifiable health intervention for most smokers — vitamin D
      correction complements but does not substitute for it.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-smoking" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
